# Comprehensive Topics A (scanned PDF, hand-transcribed from OCR + page images; answers from the printed key table).
# Q45 (rhythm strip image) is left out because the picture cannot be shown in the app.
T = [
(1,"The nurse is assisting with discharge planning for assigned clients. Which of the following referrals is appropriate for the nurse to make?",
 ["A social worker referral for a client who has diabetes mellitus (type 2) and has neuropathy.","A physical therapy (PT) referral for a client who had a total knee replacement (TKR) and needs instruction on the use of a walker.","An occupational therapy (OT) referral for a client who had an amputation and requires long-term IV antibiotics.","A speech therapy referral for a client who is scheduled to undergo an outpatient esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD)."],"B","Physical therapy assists clients with ambulation devices like a walker after a total knee replacement (TKR)."),
(2,"The nurse has attended a continuing education program on legal issues in nursing practice. Which of the following statements by the nurse indicates a correct understanding of the conference?",
 ["“Failure to communicate concerns about an older adult client who is confused and is being discharged home is an example of neglect.”","“Giving clients false assurance about a health care condition or expected outcome is an example of libel.”","“Threatening to touch a client without the client’s consent is an example of battery.”","“Posting information about a client on social media without the client’s permission is an example of slander.”"],"A","Neglect includes failure to communicate necessary client concerns, especially safety issues."),
(3,"The nurse working in a medical-surgical unit is caring for assigned clients. Which of the following tasks is appropriate to delegate?",
 ["Asking an unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP) to turn off the tube feeding for a client who is receiving continuous tube feedings and is vomiting.","Asking a licensed practical nurse (LPN/VN) to obtain a capillary blood glucose reading on a client who has become diaphoretic.","Asking an unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP) to flush the closed-wound drainage system (JP) drain of a client who is 2 days postoperative from an open appendectomy.","Asking a licensed practical nurse (LPN/VN) to teach a client who has a newly created ileostomy how to change the appliance."],"B","LPN/VNs can obtain capillary blood glucose readings. Teaching and flushing drains require an RN."),
(4,"The nurse working on the pediatric unit is caring for the following clients. It is most appropriate for the nurse to initiate an interdisciplinary conference for a client who is:",
 ["7 months old, has intussusception, is vomiting, has colicky abdominal pain, and is having jelly-like stools.","6 months old, has respiratory syncytial virus (RSV), is wheezing, and has copious nasal discharge.","2 years old, has impaired fine and gross motor skills, and was just diagnosed with cerebral palsy.","2 months old, has developmental dysplasia of the hip, unequal leg lengths, internal abduction of the left hip, and asymmetry of the gluteal folds."],"C","Cerebral palsy requires multidisciplinary planning early for coordination of therapies."),
(5,"The nurse working on the medical-surgical unit is caring for assigned clients. The nurse should initially assess the client who:",
 ["Has pericarditis with a temperature (T) of 101.8 F and chest pain that is relieved when the client leans forward.","Has atrial fibrillation (AFib) with a pulse (P) that varies from 110 to 140 and is refusing to take the scheduled dose of warfarin.","Has status asthmaticus, had wheezing on inspiration and expiration upon admission 30 minutes ago, received a nebulizer treatment, and now has vesicular breath sounds.","Had thoracic surgery 12 hours ago with a chest tube to water seal drainage and has drained 175 mL of serous sanguineous fluid in the past hour."],"D","Chest tube drainage of 175 mL in 1 hour is excessive and could indicate hemorrhage."),
(6,"The nurse is caring for a client who weighs 150 lbs (68 kg) and has developed hepatic encephalopathy. It is a priority for the nurse to notify the primary health care provider (PHCP) if the client develops:",
 ["The development of flaccidity to the lower extremities.","An increase in urine output to 1,450 mL/day.","A change in pupil size from 7 to 5 mm.","An increase in alanine aminotransferase (ALT) levels."],"A","Flaccidity indicates worsening encephalopathy and possible coma. Immediate action needed."),
(7,"The nurse working in the emergency department (ED) has received the following prescriptions for newly admitted clients. Which prescription should the nurse implement first?",
 ["Initiate an IV of 0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline) for a client who was admitted with a urinary tract infection (UTI) and has a blood pressure (BP) of 122/68 mm Hg.","Administer a second unit of packed red blood cells (PRBCs) to a client who has a hemoglobin (Hgb) of 8 mg/dL.","Administer labetalol IV to a client who has a blood pressure (BP) of 192/98 mm Hg and a pulse (P) of 138.","Administer lactulose to a client who has portal hypertension and is reporting nausea."],"C","Hypertensive emergency (BP 192/98 with tachycardia) requires priority intervention with labetalol."),
(8,"The nurse is caring for a teenage client who has chronic streptococcal infections of the throat and is now reporting swelling around the eyes. Which of the following is the priority action for the nurse?",
 ["Test the urine for glucose and ketones.","Administer an antihistamine to decrease swelling.","Inspect for generalized edema.","Check the blood pressure for hypotension."],"D","Periorbital swelling could indicate acute glomerulonephritis; monitor blood pressure first. (Source wording says “hypotension”; glomerulonephritis normally causes high blood pressure.)"),
(9,"The nurse preceptor is observing care provided by a newly hired nurse. The nurse preceptor identifies that follow-up is needed if the newly hired nurse:",
 ["Preps an IV insertion site with alcohol using a back-and-forth pattern.","Primes IV tubing with 0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline) before starting a blood transfusion.","Uses clean gloves when collecting a 24-hour urine specimen.","Administers an influenza vaccine using the Z-track method."],"D","Influenza vaccine should be IM, not using the Z-track method. Z-track is for irritating IM injections."),
(10,"The nurse manager has provided a continuing education session to nursing staff regarding client rights and responsibilities. Which of the following statements by a participant indicates a correct understanding of the teaching?",
 ["“Clients who have a mental health diagnosis waive the right to confidentiality once hospitalized.”","“Clients are not allowed to review their medical record during hospitalization.”","“Clients will be provided a list of home health agencies to contact for any ordered follow-up care.”","“Clients have a right to know about alternatives to treatment and hospitalization.”"],"D","Clients have the right to know alternatives to treatment and hospitalization."),
(11,"The nurse is caring for a child who has impetigo and has provided home instructions to the parents. Which of the following should the nurse include in the instructions?",
 ["Towels should be washed separately from other family members.","Seal non-washable items in an airtight plastic bag for 14 days.","Soak combs and hairbrushes in boiling water for 10 minutes.","Spray household surfaces with an over-the-counter (OTC) insecticide."],"A","Separate towels prevent the spread of impetigo, which is highly contagious."),
(12,"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly hired nurse care for the following assigned clients who have restraints in place. Which client situation requires immediate follow-up by the nurse preceptor?",
 ["The client whose wrist restraints are removed every 4 hours.","The client whose safety vest is tied in a half bow to the bedframe.","The client who received alprazolam and has all 4 side rails up.","The client whose roll belt is loose enough to allow for movement in the bed."],"C","Alprazolam (sedative) with 4 side rails up is a restraint without an order and needs immediate intervention."),
(13,"The nurse is caring for a client and administers the wrong drug. Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?",
 ["Determine if the client is experiencing any adverse effects.","Notify the client’s primary health care provider (PHCP).","Complete an incident report.","Inform the client to report the development of any unusual symptoms."],"A","First, assess for harm to the client after a medication error."),
(14,"The nurse is assessing clients at the scene of a mass casualty disaster and prioritizing care based on the disaster triage tag system. Which of the following clients should be transported to the healthcare facility first?",
 ["The client who has a closed fracture to the leg and a 2+ pedal pulse.","The client who is confused and has an open wound on the right lower leg.","The client who has burns to the anterior chest and neck.","The client who has a head injury with sluggish pupil response and is apneic."],"C","Burns to chest and neck compromise the airway; priority in disaster triage."),
(15,"The nurse is discussing the plan of care for a client who has disseminated herpes zoster. It is appropriate for the nurse to state:",
 ["“I will use both airborne and contact precautions when caring for the client.”","“I do not need to wear a mask when caring for this client because I have had the varicella vaccine.”","“I will inform the client’s visitors that they can only be in the room for 30 minutes per visit.”","“I will remind the client’s family not to bring in food from home.”"],"A","Disseminated herpes zoster requires both airborne and contact precautions."),
(16,"The nurse has been made aware of the following situations. The nurse should complete an incident report for which situation?",
 ["A nurse has a client who was admitted with jewelry and wants to keep the jewelry with them during their stay.","A licensed practical/vocational nurse (LPN/VN) has a client who refuses to participate in their physical therapy (PT) session.","An unlicensed assistive personnel (UAP) identifies that the blood pressure machine in a client’s room does not work correctly.","A nurse realizes they missed giving a medication 2 hours ago and proceeds with administering the medication."],"D","Medication errors (missed/delayed medication) require an incident report."),
(17,"The nurse working on a surgical unit is assessing clients for the risk of developing heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT). Which of the following clients should the nurse identify as being at greatest risk?",
 ["65-year-old male who is taking enoxaparin for the treatment of a deep vein thrombosis (DVT).","45-year-old female who has had an anaphylactic reaction following a transfusion of packed red blood cells (PRBCs).","A client who has been receiving heparin 5,000 units twice per day for the past 10 days.","20-year-old African male who has received cryoprecipitate for the treatment of hemophilia."],"C","Long-term subcutaneous heparin therapy increases risk of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT)."),
(18,"The nurse is caring for an antepartum client whose laboratory test results reveal a positive rubella titer. The client asks the nurse what this test result means. Which of the following statements is correct for the nurse to make to the client?",
 ["“You are not experiencing an infection caused by rubella at this time.”","“You will need to receive the rubella immunization at your next prenatal visit.”","“You will require the rubella immunization following the delivery of your baby.”","“You are immune to the rubella virus and can fight off exposure to the virus.”"],"D","A positive rubella titer means immunity; no immediate action needed."),
(19,"The nurse is performing a pre-college physical assessment on a client who is 18 years old. Which of the following immunizations should the nurse be prepared to administer to the client?",
 ["Varicella vaccine.","Pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine.","Meningococcal polysaccharide vaccine.","Hepatitis B vaccine."],"C","Meningococcal vaccine is critical for college-aged students living in close quarters."),
(20,"The nurse has provided discharge teaching to a client who has a fiberglass cast to the lower extremity. Which of the following indicates a correct understanding of the teaching?",
 ["“I can expect to have numbness of the toes in my left foot for the first 24 hours.”","“I should report any warm areas of the cast and odor to my doctor.”","“I should avoid applying ice to my left lower leg.”","“I can resume normal activities immediately as long as I use my crutches.”"],"B","Warm areas and odor indicate infection under the cast; requires reporting."),
(21,"The nurse is developing a plan of care for a client who has bipolar disorder and is experiencing severe depression. Which of the following is a priority nursing intervention?",
 ["Improve the client’s interactions.","Restore the client’s nutritional status.","Promote sufficient stimulation for the client.","Set firm limits and boundaries for the client."],"B","Nutrition is vital during severe depression to prevent physical decline."),
(22,"The nurse is caring for a client who was recently diagnosed with terminal cancer. Which of the following client statements indicates the client is in the denial stage of grieving?",
 ["“I am looking to see if there are any cancer treatment trials that I can enter.”","“I have been such a good person, and this is what God does to me.”","“I just want to be alive long enough to see my son graduate from college.”","“I want to get another opinion from the cancer institute.”"],"D","Denial often includes seeking a second opinion despite diagnosis."),
(23,"The nurse is caring for a newly admitted client who has obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?",
 ["Provide a structured activity schedule for the client to help prevent compulsive behavior.","Instruct the client on the use of relaxation techniques to help decrease anxiety.","Identify precipitating factors for ritualistic behaviors.","Discuss alternative coping strategies with the client."],"C","Identifying triggers for rituals comes before intervention strategies."),
(24,"The nurse working on a crisis hotline is speaking with a client who states, “I just took an entire bottle of amitriptyline.” Which of the following responses should the nurse initially make to the client?",
 ["“It must have been difficult to call and report an attempted overdose.”","“Were you trying to kill yourself by taking an overdose?”","“What was happening at the time you decided to take the pills?”","“I’m glad you called, and I want to send an ambulance to help you.”"],"D","Immediate safety is the priority; send an ambulance right away."),
(25,"The nurse is preparing to transfer a client from the bed to the chair. Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?",
 ["Assist the client to a seated position.","Position the bed at an appropriate height.","Determine if the client can bear weight.","Place a transfer belt on the client."],"C","Weight-bearing status must be determined first before transferring a client."),
(26,"The nurse working on a medical-surgical unit is caring for assigned clients. When considering dietary requirements, the nurse should recognize that a client who has:",
 ["Dumping syndrome should consume a low-protein diet.","Crohn’s disease should consume a high-residue diet.","Peptic ulcer disease (PUD) should drink fluids 1 hour after eating.","Diverticulosis should consume a high-fiber diet."],"D","Clients with diverticulosis need a high-fiber diet to prevent complications."),
(27,"The nurse is caring for a client who is receiving chemotherapy. The client is not eating well but denies any nausea or vomiting. Which of the following meals is best for the nurse to suggest to the client?",
 ["Baked pork chop and potato with cheesecake.","Dry toast, yogurt, and gelatin.","Oat bran cereal with milk and strawberries.","Broiled chicken, green beans, and cottage cheese."],"D","Broiled chicken, green beans, and cottage cheese are high protein and easy to digest."),
(28,"The nurse has instructed a client who recently received hearing aids. Which of the following client statements indicates a correct understanding of the teaching?",
 ["“I will turn the hearing aid off before removing it from my ear.”","“I will buy batteries only as I need them to prevent them from expiring.”","“I will use a toothpick to clean debris from the opening of the hearing aids.”","“I will keep the volume at the highest setting possible to avoid any feedback sounds.”"],"A","Turn off hearing aids before removing to protect the device."),
(29,"The nurse working on a telemetry unit is caring for a client who has unstable angina and is reporting chest pain with a severity of 6 on a 0 (no pain) to 10 (severe pain) scale. The nurse administers a sublingual nitroglycerin tablet. After 5 minutes, the client states the chest pain is now a severity of 3. Which of the following actions should the nurse be prepared to take?",
 ["Notify the primary health care provider (PHCP) that the client’s pain has decreased.","Apply oxygen at 2 L per nasal cannula.","Administer another sublingual nitroglycerin tablet.","Obtain an electrocardiogram (ECG) stat."],"C","If chest pain persists after 5 minutes, administer another nitroglycerin dose."),
(30,"The nurse working in an assisted living facility is caring for the following assigned clients who have mental health disorders. The nurse should immediately report to the primary health care provider (PHCP) the client who is taking:",
 ["Venlafaxine and experiencing daytime drowsiness.","Aripiprazole and experiencing dizziness when standing up.","Clozapine and having flu-like symptoms.","Fluphenazine and reporting nausea and vomiting."],"C","Flu-like symptoms with clozapine may indicate agranulocytosis, which is life-threatening."),
(31,"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly licensed nurse prepare to insert a peripheral IV on an assigned client. Which of the following actions by the newly licensed nurse requires the nurse preceptor to intervene?",
 ["Stabilizing the vein below the area where the IV catheter will be inserted.","Applying pressure above the insertion site while retracting the stylet.","Removing the tourniquet once blood is observed in the flashback chamber.","Preparing the left forearm of a client who had a left mastectomy 5 years ago."],"D","Never use an arm with a history of mastectomy for IV insertion."),
(32,"The nurse is caring for a client who is receiving IV magnesium sulfate. Which of the following client findings indicates the client is experiencing a side effect of the medication?",
 ["A change in blood pressure (BP) from 128/76 to 108/66 mm Hg.","A urinary output of 125 mL over the past 4 hours.","A respiratory rate (RR) of 12.","An elevated calcium level."],"C","A respiratory rate of 12 is low; magnesium toxicity causes respiratory depression."),
(33,"The nurse has received a hand-off report on a client who is receiving nitroglycerin 50 mg/250 mL 5% dextrose in water at a rate of 20 mcg/min. The nurse observes that the client’s IV pump is set at a rate of 3 mL/hr. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?",
 ["Increase the infusion rate to 6 mL/hr.","Decrease the infusion rate to 2 mL/hr.","Request the pharmacy to send nitroglycerin 25 mg/250 mL 5% dextrose in water.","Continue the infusion at the rate of 3 mL/hr."],"A","The rate should be 6 mL/hr (50 mg/250 mL is 200 mcg/mL, so 20 mcg/min is 1,200 mcg/hr, or 6 mL/hr)."),
(34,"The nurse is caring for a client who is receiving total parenteral nutrition (TPN). Which of the following requires follow-up by the nurse?",
 ["The client is receiving 10% dextrose in water (D10W) until a new TPN bag is delivered to the unit.","The client has experienced a weight gain of 1 lb in 48 hours.","The client’s TPN has been infusing continuously for 8 hours.","The client’s temperature (T) is greater than 100.8 F orally."],"D","Fever (100.8°F) in a client on TPN can signal sepsis and requires urgent evaluation."),
(35,"The nurse is caring for a client who is receiving desmopressin acetate. Which of the following findings indicates a therapeutic effect?",
 ["An increase in blood pressure (BP) from 88/62 to 120/72 mm Hg.","A decrease in urine output from 5,000 to 2,000 mL in 24 hours.","An increase in serum glucose from 50 to 110 mg/dL.","A decrease in apical pulse (P) rate from 120 to 86."],"B","Desmopressin decreases urine output in conditions like diabetes insipidus."),
(36,"The nurse is caring for a client who has an infection and has been prescribed gentamicin intermittent IV bolus every 8 hours. A peak and trough level is required with the next dose. Complete the following sentence using the options below. The nurse should draw the peak ____ (Option 1) after completing the infusion and the trough ____ (Option 2) prior to the infusion. Option 1 / Option 2 choices: 1. Immediately. 2. 15 minutes. 3. 30 minutes. 4. 1 hour.",
 ["3; 1.","4; 2.","2; 3.","1; 4."],"A","Peak levels should be drawn 30 minutes after, and trough levels immediately before the infusion."),
(37,"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly hired nurse insert an indwelling catheter into a female. Which of the following actions performed by the newly hired nurse requires follow-up by the preceptor?",
 ["Inflating the catheter balloon as soon as a return of urine is noted in the tubing.","Selecting a 16F catheter with a 10-mL balloon.","Cleaning the meatus with an antiseptic swab after cleaning both labia folds.","Immediately obtaining a urine specimen from the drainage bag."],"A","Balloon inflation should only occur after confirming the catheter is placed in the bladder."),
(38,"The nurse preceptor is observing a newly hired nurse care for a client who had a bronchoscopy 1 hour ago. Which of the following actions by the newly hired nurse requires follow-up?",
 ["Maintaining the client in a semi-Fowler’s position.","Encouraging the client to cough and deep breathe every 30 minutes.","Assessing the face and neck for development of crepitus.","Monitoring the client for any hemoptysis."],"B","Coughing immediately after bronchoscopy risks aspiration; the nurse must assess gag reflex first."),
(39,"The nurse is caring for a client who had abdominal surgery with placement of a nasogastric (NG) tube 3 days ago. The client has a pulse (P) of 110 and is reporting muscle cramps with tingling in the legs. Which of the following arterial blood gas (ABG) results are consistent with this client’s current assessment?",
 ["pH = 7.36; PaO2 = 63 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 42 mm Hg; HCO3 = 26 mEq/L; SaO2 = 84%.","pH = 7.45; PaO2 = 70 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 38 mm Hg; HCO3 = 28 mEq/L; SaO2 = 86%.","pH = 7.50; PaO2 = 79 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 35 mm Hg; HCO3 = 30 mEq/L; SaO2 = 88%.","pH = 7.32; PaO2 = 72 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 50 mm Hg; HCO3 = 22 mEq/L; SaO2 = 85%."],"C","Alkalosis (high pH 7.50) from NG suction causes muscle cramps and tingling; matches ABGs."),
(40,"The nurse working in the post anesthesia care unit (PACU) of an ambulatory surgery center has just received a postoperative hand-off report on a client who had endoscopic carpal tunnel release (ECTR) surgery. Which of the following actions should the nurse take first?",
 ["Determine if the client needs pain medication.","Check the surgical dressing.","Check the client’s vital signs (VS).","Auscultate the client’s breath sounds."],"C","Vital signs must be checked first postoperatively to assess for immediate complications."),
(41,"The nurse is caring for a client who has been taking a large amount of antacids. Which of the following arterial blood gas (ABG) results should the nurse expect the client to have?",
 ["pH = 7.24; PaO2 = 80 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 50 mm Hg; HCO3 = 24 mEq/L; SaO2 = 89%.","pH = 7.47; PaO2 = 85 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 38 mm Hg; HCO3 = 32 mEq/L; SaO2 = 92%.","pH = 7.36; PaO2 = 93 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 42 mm Hg; HCO3 = 26 mEq/L; SaO2 = 86%.","pH = 7.25; PaO2 = 90 mm Hg; PaCO2 = 35 mm Hg; HCO3 = 14 mEq/L; SaO2 = 95%."],"B","Excessive antacids cause metabolic alkalosis: high pH and high bicarbonate (HCO3)."),
(42,"The nurse is caring for a client who has hypovolemic shock and has a pulse (P) of 116. The nurse has initiated IV 0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline). Which of the following actions should the nurse perform next?",
 ["Obtain a prescription for a vasodilator.","Document and continue to monitor the client.","Insert an indwelling urethral catheter.","Initiate oxygen at 2 L/min via nasal cannula."],"D","Oxygenation is the immediate intervention for hypovolemic shock."),
(43,"The nurse is caring for a client who is in labor and has developed the following manifestations: pallor, dizziness, breathlessness, P 115, BP 94/58 mm Hg, and fetal distress. Which of the following actions should the nurse take immediately?",
 ["Prepare the client for surgery.","Administer oxygen at 2 L/minute via nasal cannula.","Initiate an IV infusion of hypertonic fluids.","Turn the client to the left side."],"D","Left side positioning improves uteroplacental blood flow and relieves fetal distress."),
(44,"The nurse is assessing an infant who has hyperbilirubinemia and is receiving phototherapy. Which of the following findings indicates an adverse response to the therapy?",
 ["Lanugo to the back and shoulders.","Urine output that has a dark-gold or brown appearance.","An increased amount of loose, green stools.","Fontanels that are sunken upon palpation."],"D","Sunken fontanels suggest dehydration, a serious adverse effect of phototherapy."),
(46,"The nurse is planning care for a client who is on bedrest. The client has end-stage cirrhosis of the liver with hepatorenal failure and abdominal ascites. Which of the following interventions should the nurse be prepared to implement?",
 ["Provide the client a low-sodium diet.","Administer vitamin K.","Encourage the client to cough and deep breathe every hour.","Administer acetaminophen for pain."],"A","Low-sodium diet is critical for clients with ascites to reduce fluid retention. Sodium restriction helps manage fluid accumulation."),
(47,"The nurse is talking with a client who has multiple sclerosis (MS). Which of the following statements by the client indicates correct understanding of the condition?",
 ["“I participate in high-impact aerobics at least 5 days a week.”","“I take a hot bath every evening as a way to relax.”","“I try and drink at least 2 liters of fluid every day.”","“I eat plenty of low-fiber, low-potassium foods every day.”"],"C","Increasing fluid intake is important for clients with MS to prevent urinary tract infections and maintain hydration. High-impact exercise and hot baths can worsen fatigue and symptoms."),
(48,"The nurse is performing an initial assessment on a client who had a colostomy created 6 hours ago. The colostomy stoma is dark, red, and flaccid. The only drainage in the ostomy bag is a small amount of mucus. Which of the following actions is a priority for the nurse to take?",
 ["Notify the surgeon of the findings.","Determine if the client has been passing flatus.","Assess the client’s bowel sounds.","Ensure proper placement of the pouch."],"A","A dark, flaccid stoma suggests compromised blood supply (ischemia) and is a surgical emergency. Healthy stomas should be pink or beefy red and moist."),
(49,"The nurse is caring for a client who recently sustained a head injury. The client has the following data: Potassium 3.5 mEq/L; Sodium 110 mEq/L; Serum osmolality decreased; Urine output 20 mL/hr, tea-colored; Urine specific gravity increased; Lethargy, disorientation, headache; Loss of appetite, nausea; Exertional dyspnea; Deep tendon reflexes decreased; Apical P 110, bounding; T 98 F. Which of the following actions should the nurse take?",
 ["Administer IV vasopressin.","Raise the head of bed (HOB) to high-Fowler’s.","Start an infusion of 0.45% sodium chloride (half-strength saline).","Provide a calm and quiet environment."],"D","The client shows signs of SIADH (low sodium, concentrated urine, decreased serum osmolality, lethargy). The priority is to reduce stimulation by providing a calm and quiet environment."),
(50,"The nurse is caring for a client who had lumbar spinal surgery 8 hours ago. The client reports development of a headache and the nurse notes clear drainage on the surgical dressing. The nurse should immediately:",
 ["Lower the client’s head of bed (HOB).","Empty the Hemovac drain.","Remove the sequential compression devices.","Reposition the client using the log rolling method."],"A","Clear drainage from the surgical site after spinal surgery suggests a possible cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) leak. Lowering the head of the bed reduces intracranial pressure and decreases further leakage while waiting for provider intervention."),
]
